 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Use",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Built for low loss in power conversion: primary-side switching in telecom supplies, secondary-side synchronous rectifiers and motor drives. Gate charge (Qg and Qgd) and thermal resistance are both kept low."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Gate drive",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Threshold lies between 2.2 and 3.3 V at 250 µA. With 16 A flowing, on-resistance is at most 7.8 mΩ at 6 V of drive and 6.4 mΩ at 10 V. Taking the gate to 10 V from a 50 V drain at 16 A needs 37 nC typical, 48 nC max. The gate's internal series resistance is 1.3 Ω typical, 2.6 Ω max. In Figure 7 on-resistance climbs steeply once VGS drops below roughly 5 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Current limits",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The package holds continuous drain current to 100 A; the die alone would carry 110 A with the case at 25 °C. On a 1 in² copper pad with a typical 40 °C/W to ambient the continuous rating is 16 A and dissipation 3.3 W. Pulses reach 337 A. Figure 12 keeps 100 A up to about 90 °C case and falls to zero at 150 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Temperature",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "On-resistance grows with case temperature: against its 25 °C value it is about 0.6 at −55 °C and about 1.9 to 2.0 at 150 °C (Figure 8). Threshold drops from about 3.1 V at −55 °C to about 1.8 V at 150 °C (Figure 6)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Avalanche",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "A single avalanche pulse of 60 A peak into 0.1 mH with 25 Ω gate resistance is rated at 180 mJ. Figure 11 plots peak avalanche current against time in avalanche, from starting case temperatures of 25 °C and 125 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Body diode",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "The body diode drops 0.8 V typical, 1 V max, carrying 16 A. Recovering from 16 A at 300 A/µs against 50 V takes 148 ns and moves 226 nC."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Heat",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Junction to case is at most 1 °C/W. Junction to ambient is at most 50 °C/W with 1 in² of 2 oz copper on the drain and 115 °C/W on the minimum pad; RθJA belongs to the user's board."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Handling",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "The gate has little ESD protection of its own. Keep the leads shorted together or the part in conductive foam while it is stored or handled."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Moisture and reflow",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "MSL 1 at a 260 °C peak, with unlimited floor life; leads finished in tin (SN)."
  }
 ],
